> 1) I can figure out how to invert the endianness of the recover
> utility.  I'm not sure how to do that, but if anybody can offer
> guidance that would be great.

You could use the network byte order conversion functions ntohl(),
htonl() and friends when reading in ints instead of doing block
copies.  Network ordering is big-endian, and if you're on a
little-endian, they'll convert between them.  It'll at least save
some time over rolling your own.

The problem with this is you'll need to know not only the
types but also the size of each int, so you know whether to use
the short or long versions. (It'll probably always be long if
you're on a 32 bit machine.) And since recover just seems to
do a block copy on most things, you'd need to mess with restore.c
itself in all likelihood.

> 3) I can get a copy of the recover utility that matches my nethack
> installation, and presumably that would already match endianness with
> its sibling.
> 
> I don't know what causes the endian disagreement, but I'm suspicious
> that the Terminal port mentioned above is compiled for PPC instead of
> Intel (which disagree on endianness).

Wait, which system are you on now and is it the same one the save was
created on?  If you've compiled the PPC version and you're on an
Intel box, endianness should be the least of your worries; those don't
even have the same instruction set.  It shouldn't even RUN if that's
the issue.

If you're running MacOS X on an Intel machine, you might try compiling
a plain Unix version. If the Terminal port only changed I/O, it might
not have affected savefile format.

Be sure you back up the level files before you do anything else.
Recover will delete them if it thinks it was successful, even if a
problem surfaces when you try to restore the save.


